{"name":"Whale Watching","hex":"#a5a495","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"whale watching","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The practice of observing whales and other cetaceans in their natural habitat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/whale_watching"}],"entries":[{"title":"Whale watching","blocks":[{"text":"Whale watching is the practice of observing whales, dolphins and porpoises (cetaceans) in their natural habitat. Whale watching is mostly a recreational activity, but it can also serve scientific and/or educational purposes. A study prepared for International Fund for Animal Welfare in 2009 estimated that 13 million people went whale watching globally in 2008. Whale watching generates $2.1 billion per annum in tourism revenue worldwide, employing around 13,000 workers. The size and rapid growth of the industry has led to complex and continuing debates with the whaling industry about the best use of whales as a natural resource."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Whale_Watching.jpg/330px-Whale_Watching.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whale_watching","wikidata":"Q1366963"},{"title":"Whale","blocks":[{"text":"Whales are a widely distributed and diverse group of fully aquatic placental marine mammals. As an informal and colloquial grouping, they correspond to large members of the infraorder Cetacea, i.e. all cetaceans apart from dolphins and porpoises. Dolphins and porpoises may be considered whales from a formal, cladistic perspective. Whales, dolphins and porpoises belong to the order Cetartiodactyla, which consists of even-toed ungulates. Their closest non-cetacean living relatives are the hippopotamuses, from which they and other cetaceans diverged about 54 million years ago. The two parvorders of whales, baleen whales (Mysticeti) and toothed whales (Odontoceti), are thought to have had their last common ancestor around 34 million years ago. Mysticetes include four extant (living) families: Balaenopteridae, Balaenidae, Cetotheriidae, and Eschrichtiidae. Odontocetes include the Monodontidae, Physeteridae, Kogiidae, and Ziphiidae, as well as the six families of dolphins and porpoises which are not considered whales in the informal sense."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Southern_right_whale.jpg/330px-Southern_right_whale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whale","wikidata":"Q1865281"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/MBNMS_kayaker_too_close_to_humpback_whale_%2849040807441%29.jpg/500px-MBNMS_kayaker_too_close_to_humpback_whale_%2849040807441%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/MBNMS_kayaker_too_close_to_humpback_whale_%2849040807441%29.jpg/1280px-MBNMS_kayaker_too_close_to_humpback_whale_%2849040807441%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/MBNMS_kayaker_too_close_to_humpback_whale_%2849040807441%29.jpg/500px-MBNMS_kayaker_too_close_to_humpback_whale_%2849040807441%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An example of being too close","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMBNMS_kayaker_too_close_to_humpback_whale_(49040807441).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Avistamiento.JPG/500px-Avistamiento.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Avistamiento.JPG/500px-Avistamiento.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Whale watching in El Vizcaíno Biospere Reserve, Baja California S